## Pattern Matching on Enums

The `match` expression is how you destructure and respond to each variant of an enum. It's **exhaustive** — the compiler checks that every variant is handled.

### Basic Match

```xiom
pub type Light = enum { Red, Yellow, Green; }

fn action(light: &Light) -> Str {
  match light {
    Red => { return "Stop"; }
    Yellow => { return "Slow"; }
    Green => { return "Go"; }
  }
}
```

### Match with Data Extraction

```xiom
pub type Result[T, E] = enum {
  Ok(value: T),
  Err(error: E),
}

fn unwrap_or_default[T](r: &Result[T, Int]) -> T {
  match r {
    Ok(value) => { return value; }
    Err(_) => { /* handle error */ }
  }
}
```

### Match as Expression

Match returns a value — you can assign its result:

```xiom
let description = match light {
  Red => "Stop",
  Yellow => "Slow",
  Green => "Go",
};
```

### Exhaustiveness Check

This **won't compile** — missing a variant:

```xiom
match light {
  Red => { return "Stop"; }
  Green => { return "Go"; }
  // ERROR: missing variant Yellow
}
```

### Match with Multiple Arms

You can merge multiple variants with `|`:

```xiom
match light {
  Red | Yellow => { return "Don't Go"; }
  Green => { return "Go"; }
}
```

### Nested Match Arms

```xiom
pub type Shape = enum {
  Circle(radius: Float64),
  Rect(w: Float64, h: Float64),
}

fn is_large(s: &Shape) -> Bool {
  match s {
    Circle(radius) => { return radius > 10.0; }
    Rect(w, h) => { return w > 10.0 || h > 10.0; }
  }
}
```

